p/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-b71fffff5dcb3d50.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 150px; height: 139px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-b71fffff5dcb3d50.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-b71fffff5dcb3d50.png\"></p>","<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-85ecab747f220e61.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 135px; height: 139px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-85ecab747f220e61.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-85ecab747f220e61.png\"></p>","<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-97908d62fb2bf730.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 185px; height: 103px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-97908d62fb2bf730.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-97908d62fb2bf730.png\"></p>"],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrocarbons","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quantrex PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-913fc78cce07d1d1.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 560px; height: 100px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-913fc78cce07d1d1.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-913fc78cce07d1d1.png\"></p>It is& oxymercuration−demercuration reaction. In this an alkene is treated with mercury(II) acetate,& and the product is treated with sodium borohydride. The net result is a Markovnikov addition product without rearrangement.","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f57e39d99b57bc5b4d"},{"id":"qz_69de4f7f2ee0e063d928f997","_quizrrId":"69de4f7f2ee0e063d928f997","q":"Polysubstitution is a major drawback in:","options":["Reimer Tiemann reaction","Friedel Craft's alkylation","Acetylation of aniline","Friedel Craft's acylation"],"answer":1,"answers":[1],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrocarbons","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quantrex PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-8be91dc699a2be5c.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-8be91dc699a2be5c.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-8be91dc699a2be5c.png\"><br />In Friedel crafts alkylation product obtained is more activated due to the activating nature of the alkyl group and hence poly substitution will take place.","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f57e39d99b57bc5b4d"},{"id":"qz_69de4f7f2ee0e063d928f99c","_quizrrId":"69de4f7f2ee0e063d928f99c","q":"<p>Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.</p><p><strong>Assertion A:& </strong><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-ac9ec6827b77be93.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 90px; height: 68px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-ac9ec6827b77be93.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-ac9ec6827b77be93.png\">& can be easily reduced using $Zn - Hg / HCl$ to~<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-f7c8159b008eed5a.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 80px; height: 52px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-f7c8159b008eed5a.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-f7c8159b008eed5a.png\"></p><p><strong>Reason R:</strong> $Zn - Hg / HCl$ is used to reduce carbonyl group to −${CH}_{2}$ − group.</p><p>In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:</p>","options":["A is false but R is true","A is true but R is false","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A"],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrocarbons","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quantrex PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p>Clemmensen reduction: The carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones is reduced to& ${CH}_{2}$~group on treatment with zinc-amalgam and concentrated hydrochloric acid.</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-9a51f8d93869a0d9.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 500px; height: 124px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-9a51f8d93869a0d9.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-9a51f8d93869a0d9.png\"></p><p>The acid sensitive alcohol group reacts with $HCl$, hence Clemmenson reduction is not suitable for above conversion.</p><p>So, assertion is false but reason is true.</p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f57e39d99b57bc5b4d"},{"id":"qz_69de4f7f2ee0e063d928f9a1","_quizrrId":"69de4f7f2ee0e063d928f9a1","q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement I : One mole of propyne reacts with excess of sodium to liberate half a mole of $\\mathrm{H}_2$ gas.\nStatement II : Four g of propyne reacts with $\\mathrm{NaNH}_2$ to liberate $\\mathrm{NH}_3$ gas which occupies 224 mL at STP.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","options":["Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"answer":2,"answers":[2],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrocarbons","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quantrex PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"$\\mathrm{CH}_3-\\mathrm{C} \\equiv \\mathrm{CH}+\\mathrm{Na} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CH}_3-\\mathrm{C} \\equiv \\mathrmC^{-} \\mathrm{Na}^{+}+\\frac{1}{2} \\mathrm{H}_2$ Statement-l is correct.\nMoles of $\\mathrm{C}_3 \\mathrm{H}_4=\\frac{4}{40}=0.1$ mole\n$\\mathrm{CH}_3-\\mathrm{C} \\equiv \\mathrm{CH}+\\mathrm{NaNH}_2 \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CH}_3-\\mathrm{C} \\equiv \\mathrmC^{-} \\mathrm{Na}^{+}+\\mathrm{NH}_3$\n0.1 mole\n0.1 mole Volume of $\\mathrm{NH}_3=(0.1)(22.4)=2.24 \\mathrm{~L}$\nStatement-II is incorrect.","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f57e39d99b57bc5b4d"},{"id":"qz_69de4f7f2ee0e063d928f9a6","_quizrrId":"69de4f7f2ee0e063d928f9a6","q":"Identify correct $A, B$ and $C$ in the reaction sequence given below :<br /><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-c0584ffc300dd24e.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 543px; height: 127px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-c0584ffc300dd24e.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-c0584ffc300dd24e.png\">","options":["<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-1703d22baa562a5a.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 588px; height: 137px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-1703d22baa562a5a.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-1703d22baa562a5a.png\">","<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-d0de1228e2d208b6.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 699px; height: 109px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-d0de1228e2d208b6.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-d0de1228e2d208b6.png\">","<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-4ef51da611a226b3.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 603px; height: 137px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-4ef51da611a226b3.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-4ef51da611a226b3.png\">","<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-cba7fea0bd4192c1.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 711px; height: 104px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-cba7fea0bd4192c1.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-cba7fea0bd4192c1.png\">"],"answer":3,"answers":[3],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrocarbons","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quantrex PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p>In first step nitration occurs by mixture of concentrated nitric acid and concentrated sulphuric acid. From this step nitrobenzene (A) is produced.</p><p>In next step chlorination of nitrobenzene occurs, nitro group is meta directing which orients the incoming electrophile at meta position which results in formation of compound (B).</p><p>In last step, reduction occurs and nitro group is converted into amino group and produces compound (C).</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-42196a34d134e3fd.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 943px; height: 124px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-42196a34d134e3fd.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-42196a34d134e3fd.png\"></p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f57e39d99b57bc5b4d"},{"id":"qz_69de4f7f2ee0e063d928f9ab","_quizrrId":"69de4f7f2ee0e063d928f9ab","q":"Which of the following potential energy $( P . E . )$ diagrams represents the ${SN}_{1}$ reaction?","options":["<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-8c3852326eae3417.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 265px; height: 152px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-8c3852326eae3417.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-8c3852326eae3417.png\">","<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-d79695a792d6fe54.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 286px; height: 164px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-d79695a792d6fe54.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-d79695a792d6fe54.png\">","<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-dfe685042d7e94ca.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 278px; height: 162px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-dfe685042d7e94ca.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-dfe685042d7e94ca.png\">","<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-3840a6532012ffb6.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 289px; height: 168px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-3840a6532012ffb6.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-3840a6532012ffb6.png\">"],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrocarbons","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quantrex PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"$P . E .$ diagram for ${SN}_{1}$<br /><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-6f83525fb802276b.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 283px; height: 193px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-6f83525fb802276b.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-6f83525fb802276b.png\"><br />${SN}_{1}$ is two step reaction where in step  \\left(1\\right)$ formation of carbocation is $RDS$.$","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f57e39d99b57bc5b4d","_resolvedFrom":"quizrr_submit"},{"id":"qz_69de4f7f2ee0e063d928f9b0","_quizrrId":"69de4f7f2ee0e063d928f9b0","q":"Choose the correct set of reagents for the following conversion<br />trans  \\left(Ph - CH = CH - {CH}_{3}\\right) \\to \\text{cis}\\left(Ph - CH = CH - {CH}_{3}\\right)","options":["${Br}_{2}, $\\text{alc}$KOH, {NaNH}_{2}, Na  \\left({LiqNH}_{3}\\right)$","<p>${Br}_{2}\\text{, alc}KOH, {NaNH}_{2}, {H}_{2}$</p><p>Lindlar Catalyst</p>","${Br}_{2}, aqKOH, {NaNH}_{2}, {H}_{2}$& Lindlar Catalyst","${Br}_{2}$, aq $KOH, {NaNH}_{2}, Na  \\left({LiqNH}_{3}\\right)$"],"answer":1,"answers":[1],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrocarbons","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quantrex PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p>A trans alkene can be converted to a vicinal dihalide through a halogenation reaction. The vicinal dihalide can undergo double elimination to afford an alkyne. The alkyne can undergo partial reduction to give a cis alkene.</p><p>The reagents needed to convert& trans<i>$( Ph - CH = CH - {CH}_{3} )$</i>~to~cis-$( Ph - CH = CH - {CH}_{3} )$& are shown in the reaction scheme below.</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-9f6851116640f5db.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 600px; height: 431px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-9f6851116640f5db.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-9f6851116640f5db.png\"></p><p></p><p></p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f57e39d99b57bc5b4d"},{"id":"qz_69de4f7f2ee0e063d928f9b5","_quizrrId":"69de4f7f2ee0e063d928f9b5","q":"<p>Consider the following reactions:<br />\\left(a\\right){\\left(C{H}_{3}\\right)}_{3}CCH\\left(OH\\right)C{H}_{3}\\overset{conc . {H}_{2}S{O}_{4}}{\\to}$<br />\\left(b\\right){\\left(C{H}_{3}\\right)}_{2}CHCH\\left(Br\\right)C{H}_{3}\\overset{alc . KOH}{\\to}$<br />\\left(c\\right){\\left(C{H}_{3}\\right)}_{2}CHCH  \\left(Br\\right)C{H}_{3}\\overset{{ \\left($C{H}_{3}\\right)}_{3}O^{⊖}K^{⊕}}{\\to}$<br />\\left($d\\right) <img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-bbc977aec99a78cb.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-bbc977aec99a78cb.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-bbc977aec99a78cb.png\"></p><p>Which of the reaction(s) will not produce Saytzeff product?</p>$","options":["\\left(a\\right),  \\left(c\\right)$ and  \\left(d\\right)$","  \\left(d\\right)$ only$","  \\left(c\\right)$ only$","  \\left(b\\right)$ and  \\left(d\\right)$"],"answer":2,"answers":[2],"questionType":"singleCorrect","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrocarbons","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quantrex PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-5ffc630c6e7e6a61.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"height: 471px; width: 464px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-5ffc630c6e7e6a61.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-5ffc630c6e7e6a61.png\"></p><p>We have to know that a Saytzeff product is basically an alkene compound which has undergone many substitution reactions to form the alkene product. From Saytzeff rule, a stable alkene is formed when removal of hydrogen from beta carbon has a low number of hydrogen atoms.</p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f57e39d99b57bc5b4d"},{"id":"qz_69de4f7f2ee0e063d928fa6c","_quizrrId":"69de4f7f2ee0e063d928fa6c","q":"The minimum number of moles of ${O}_{2}$ required for complete combustion of 1 mole of propane and 2 moles of butane is..............","options":["","","",""],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"numerical","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrocarbons","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quantrex PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p>(1) Combustion of propane.</p><p>${C}_{3}{H}_{8} + 5{O}_{2}\\left(g\\right) \\to 3{CO}_{2}\\left(g\\right) + 4{H}_{2}O1mole5mole$</p><p>(2) Combustion of butane</p><p>${C}_{4}{H}_{10} + \\dfrac{13}{2}{O}_{2} \\to 4{CO}_{2}\\left(g\\right) + 5{H}_{2}O1mole\\dfrac{13}{2}mole$</p><p>so total mole of ${O}_{2}= 5 + 13 = 18$</p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f57e39d99b57bc5b4d","correctValue":"18.0","_resolvedFrom":"quizrr_submit"},{"id":"qz_69de4f7f2ee0e063d928fa71","_quizrrId":"69de4f7f2ee0e063d928fa71","q":"Consider the following chemical reaction.<br />$CH ≡ CH\\dfrac{1 ) RedhotFetube, 873K}{2 ) CO, HCl, {AlCl}_{3}} > Product$<br />The number of ${sp}^{2}$ hybridized carbon atom(s) present in the product is","options":["","","",""],"answer":"7","answers":["7"],"questionType":"numerical","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrocarbons","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quantrex PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fjee_main%252F5bd4a9e1-f674-4489-849f-ce84dc693f90-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=qxfig138\" style=\"width: 466px; height: 153px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-bda4d6a9e81379bc.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-bda4d6a9e81379bc.png\"></p><p>In benzaldehyde total number of ${sp}^{2}$&#39; $C '$ are $7 .$</p>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f57e39d99b57bc5b4d","correctValue":"7","_resolvedFrom":"official_bank_unique"},{"id":"qz_69de4f7f2ee0e063d928fa76","_quizrrId":"69de4f7f2ee0e063d928fa76","q":"Number of compounds from the following which cannot undergo Friedel-Crafts reactions is: ________.\ntoluene, nitrobenzene, xylene, cumene, aniline, chlorobenzene, \\(m\\)-nitroaniline, \\(m\\)-dinitrobenzene","options":["","","",""],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"numerical","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrocarbons","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quantrex PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"Compounds which can not undergo Friedel Crafts reaction are\n<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-1bba2e94129711ba.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-1bba2e94129711ba.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-1bba2e94129711ba.png\"><br>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f57e39d99b57bc5b4d","correctValue":"4.0","_resolvedFrom":"quizrr_submit"},{"id":"qz_69de4f7f2ee0e063d928fa7b","_quizrrId":"69de4f7f2ee0e063d928fa7b","q":"Molar mass of the hydrocarbon (X) which on ozonolysis consumes one mole of ${O}_{3}$& per mole of (X) and gives one mole each of ethanal and propanone is ______ g mol<sup>−1</sup> (Molar mass of C : 12 g mol<sup>−1</sup> , H : 1 g mol<sup>−1</sup> )","options":["","","",""],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"numerical","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrocarbons","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quantrex PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<blockquote><p>When 2-methyl butene-2 is subjected to ozonolysis and then reduced in presence of dimethyl sulphide or Zn dust, it yields acetone and acetaldehyde. Cycloaddition of a molecule of ozone occurs first and then it is cleaved reductively to give aldehyde and ketone.</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-304b460c02ad4f3b.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 500px; height: 140px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-304b460c02ad4f3b.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-304b460c02ad4f3b.png\"></p><p></p></blockquote>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f57e39d99b57bc5b4d","correctValue":"70.0","_resolvedFrom":"quizrr_submit"},{"id":"qz_69de4f7f2ee0e063d928fa80","_quizrrId":"69de4f7f2ee0e063d928fa80","q":"<p>In the following reaction</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-922e1b832230b3b5.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 380px; height: 130px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-922e1b832230b3b5.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-922e1b832230b3b5.png\"></p><p>The $%$ yield for reaction $I$ is $60 %$ and that of reaction II is $50 %$. The overall yield of the complete reaction is$%$</p>","options":["","","",""],"answer":0,"answers":[0],"questionType":"numerical","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Hydrocarbons","exam":"JEE Main","source":"Quantrex PYQ Chapterwise","solution":"<blockquote><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-7cdb59200eff9f43.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 281px; height: 100px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-7cdb59200eff9f43.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-7cdb59200eff9f43.png\"></p><p>Let initial moles of reactant taken $= n$<br />Total moles obtained for benzene sulphonic acid (with $%$ yield $= 60 %$)~$= 0 . 6n$</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fdiagrams%2Fqx-book-0411d4df10659286.png?alt=media&v=stem2\" style=\"width: 262px; height: 120px;\" / data-qx-orig-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-0411d4df10659286.png\" data-qx-storage-src=\"/assets/diagrams/qx-book-0411d4df10659286.png\"></p><p>Moles of benzene sulphonic acid before reaction $II = 0 . 6n$</p><p>Moles obtained for phenol (with $%$ yield = $50 %$)~$= 0 . 6 \\times 0 . 5n = 0 . 3n$</p><p>So over all $%$~yield of complete reaction~$= \\dfrac{0 . 3n}{n} \\times 100 = 30$</p></blockquote>","_bank":"quizrr_pyq_chapter","_testId":"69de21f57e39d99b57bc5b4d","correctValue":"30.0","_resolvedFrom":"quizrr_submit"}]}